INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Wirecard evidence batch: company KPMG announcement and European Parliament policy briefing", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002", "text": "SRC-001 (Wirecard/DGAP announcement dated 28 Apr 2020): Wirecard said KPMG's special investigation found no incriminating evidence for public allegations of balance-sheet forgery; across TPA, MCA/Digital Lending, India and Singapore, no significant findings requiring adjustment of 2016-2018 annual accounts. It also said KPMG could not obtain all data needed to prove earlier TPA revenues because data were controlled by third parties; more than 200 million December 2019 data sets supplied by Wirecard showed no deviations between reported revenues and account balances. KPMG identified documentation and organizational weaknesses; Wirecard said it was remedying them through a Global Compliance Organization and external consultants. EY audit completion and annual accounts release were delayed.\n\nSRC-002 (European Parliament ECON-commissioned SAFE policy briefing, manuscript completed May 2020, published November 2020): Authors assess that all five defenses failed in one way or another: Wirecard internal controls, supervisory board, external audit, oversight bodies for financial reporting/auditing, and BaFin. The briefing says warning signals existed years before revelation. It reports allegations of accounting inconsistencies from 2015, FT reporting partly based on Singapore whistleblower information, the 2016 Zatarra report alleging money laundering and fraud, BaFin referrals of some short sellers for market-abuse investigations, BaFin's 2019 request for FREP investigation after an FT report, BaFin's two-month short-selling restriction and complaint against short sellers and the FT journalist, continued FT reporting allegedly inflated sales/profits backed by internal documents, KPMG's October 2019 forensic investigation and delayed April 2020 report stating it could not verify €1 billion in foreign bank accounts, BaFin criminal complaint in June 2020, and Wirecard bankruptcy days later acknowledging €1.9 billion in missing financial assets. The briefing characterizes the root cause as a flawed market/institutional oversight architecture, while noting hindsight-bias concerns and that the authors' opinions do not necessarily represent the European Parliament's official position. The returned benchmark sources contain no evidence establishing Jan Marsalek's disappearance, whereabouts, role, or any relationship involving him."}
